  <span class="kicker">Choosing a treatment</span>
  <h1>Ayurvedic vs. sports massage: which one do you need?</h1>
  <p>Both are real massage traditions with real technique behind them — they're just built to solve different problems. Here's the actual difference, so you can book the right one instead of guessing.</p>

  <h2>Ayurvedic massage (abhyanga)</h2>
  <p>Performed with warm herbal oils, in long, rhythmic, full-body strokes. It's a relaxation and nervous-system treatment first — the goal is to calm you down, support circulation and leave your skin nourished. See <a href="what-is-abhyanga.html">what is abhyanga</a> for the full breakdown.</p>
  <p><strong>Best for:</strong> unwinding after travel, a stretch of poor sleep or stress, or simply wanting a genuinely restorative hour rather than targeted work.</p>

  <h2>Sports massage</h2>
  <p>Deep-tissue work, applied with more pressure and more precision to the areas that are actually tight — shoulders, lower back, calves, whatever your body is holding onto. The goal is recovery and function, not just relaxation.</p>
  <p><strong>Best for:</strong> athletes, surfers and anyone physically active who needs muscles to recover faster — see <a href="index.html#surf">surf recovery massage in Sagres</a> if that's you.</p>

  <h2>Side by side</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Technique</strong> — ayurvedic: warm oil, full body, rhythmic. Sports: deep tissue, targeted, firmer pressure.</li>
    <li><strong>Goal</strong> — ayurvedic: relaxation, circulation, nervous system. Sports: muscle recovery, mobility, performance.</li>
    <li><strong>Feel afterwards</strong> — ayurvedic: calm, soft, unwound. Sports: looser, sometimes a little sore, more mobile.</li>
    <li><strong>Good for</strong> — ayurvedic: anyone wanting to slow down. Sports: anyone training, surfing or physically pushing their body.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Not sure? Combine them</h2>
  <p>You don't have to pick one philosophy over the other. Shaantha's <a href="index.html#treatments">ayurvedic &amp; sports combination</a> opens the tissue with warm oils first, then goes in with deeper, targeted muscle work exactly where you need it — the most requested treatment for exactly this reason.</p>

